architecture commitment-graph
  canonical_name CommitmentGraph
  family economic
  domain Economic / Commitment
  layer economic
  semantic_question Who committed what resource to whom, under what conditions, until when -- and what is still owed after the exchange ended?
  purpose Resource commitments -- purchase orders, SLAs, equipment reservations, grants, human assignments -- each domain-distinct, with authority and expiry. It holds obligation as state rather than as an outcome, so a broken promise remains a fact about a counterparty instead of an error that was handled.
  maturity measured
  source_path engine/core/src/commitment.c
  source_path engine/core/include/bf_commitment.h
  source_path schemas/control/commitment.yaff
  source_path schemas/control/commitment.cue
  source_path components/BonfyreControlPlane/commitment_graph.py.archive
  source_path components/BonfyreControlPlane/native/commitment_cli.c
  source_path components/BonfyreControlPlane/commitment-graph.mjs
  source_path components/BonfyreControlPlane/economic_graphs.py.archive
  witness components/BonfyreControlPlane/tests/commitment-graph.test.mjs drives the native C owner and proves four forbidden inferences by falsifying them: delegation leaves two obligations, violation remains outstanding and can discharge late with its history intact, compensation requires a prior breach, and expiry lists violable work without changing state (17 tests, 37 assertions)
  witness engine/core/tests/commitment_test.c exercises the same SQLite relations through the native owner: an open promise is not delivered, expiry only nominates it as violable, delegation leaves two live obligations, compensation refuses an unbroken promise, terminality holds, and the four-event history remains append-only
  witness components/BonfyreControlPlane/tests/test_economic_graphs.py.archive
  node_types ResourceCommitment
  node_types CommitmentEvent
  edge_types commits
  edge_types delegates
  state_types open
  state_types discharged
  state_types delegated
  state_types violated
  state_types compensated
  internal_calculus state is a projection of an append-only event log rather than a column; a status that is updated in place cannot answer whether an obligation was ever violated, which is the question compensation exists to keep askable
  internal_calculus the transition table is closed and terminal states accept nothing further, so a recorded breach cannot be walked back by re-marking the row
  invariant delegation does not release the original obligor; two obligations exist afterwards and the first stays live, because the beneficiary was promised by the original party and never agreed to be handed to another
  invariant violation is not an ending: a violated commitment remains outstanding and can still be discharged late, and the violation stays in the history when it is
  invariant compensation is reachable only from violation; compensating an unbroken obligation would record a remedy for nothing and let a caller manufacture the appearance of a handled failure
  invariant every commitment carries the enactment that created it, so an obligation is traceable to the exchange that made it rather than standing free
  forbidden_inference a commitment -> the resource delivered
  forbidden_inference a delegation -> the original obligor being released
  forbidden_inference a compensation -> the violation not having happened
  forbidden_inference an expiry passing -> a violation (someone with standing must observe and record it; a clock deciding that a person broke a promise is the inference this refuses)
  forbidden_inference a settled commitment -> a reliable counterparty (discharged and compensated are both settled and only one of them was kept)
  source_lineage run6/run7 economic estate
  known_gap native C owns the state machine and shared SQLite relations; ProtocolContract and ObligationWork now bind it through the C CLI while in-process native callers use bf_commitment_* directly. The archived Python reference is no longer an active enactment path
  known_gap authority on a commitment is a recorded string rather than a checked AuthorityGraph edge, so who was entitled to delegate is not yet enforced here
  known_gap ResourceActivation is still the nearest real shape for turning a discharged commitment into an activated resource

